NASA HUNCH Raspberry Pi Server Farm

This project has two main parts:
1. `Cluster/`: monitoring and maintenance stack (Prometheus, Grafana, backend, exporters).
2. `deployer/`: generic app hosting deployer for queued app contracts.

The deployer supports:
1. Standard web/backend container apps.
2. AI apps (scheduled onto AI-labeled nodes).
3. Apps built from a Git repo containing a Dockerfile.

Presentation Hardware Layout (recommended)
1. `2 x Raspberry Pi 5 (8GB)` -> `role=ai`
2. `3 x Raspberry Pi 5 (4GB)` -> `role=general`
3. Use one 8GB Pi as the Swarm manager.

Where code/images need to exist
1. Clone the repo on the Swarm manager Pi (required).
2. The deployer and stack commands run from the manager Pi.
3. Docker images must be available on nodes that run them.
4. Public images can be pulled by workers automatically (internet required).
5. Custom local images may need to be pre-built on nodes or pushed to a registry.

Requirements / Packages

Install on all 5 Pis (required)
1. Raspberry Pi OS 64-bit (Bookworm recommended).
2. Network access on same LAN (all Pis).
3. Docker Engine:
   `curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com | sh`
4. Add user to Docker group:
   `sudo usermod -aG docker $USER`
5. System tools:
   `sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y`
   `sudo apt install -y git curl python3 python3-pip`
6. Reload shell/group (or log out/in):
   `newgrp docker`

Install on manager Pi only (required for deployer)
1. Python packages:
   `pip3 install --user pyyaml pytest`

Optional but useful on manager Pi
1. `jq` for JSON output formatting:
   `sudo apt install -y jq`

Recommended hostnames (helps your demo)
1. `pi-ai-1` (8GB, manager)
2. `pi-ai-2` (8GB, worker)
3. `pi-gen-1` (4GB, worker)
4. `pi-gen-2` (4GB, worker)
5. `pi-gen-3` (4GB, worker)

Optional hostname command (run on each Pi, reboot after):
1. `sudo hostnamectl set-hostname <new-name>`
2. `sudo reboot`

Day-Of Setup: Build the Swarm

On manager Pi (`pi-ai-1`)
1. Initialize Swarm:
   `docker swarm init`
2. Get worker join command:
   `docker swarm join-token worker`
3. Copy the printed join command.

On each worker Pi (`pi-ai-2`, `pi-gen-1`, `pi-gen-2`, `pi-gen-3`)
1. Run the join command printed by the manager:
   `docker swarm join --token <token> <manager-ip>:2377`

Back on manager Pi
4. Verify nodes:
   `docker node ls`

Node Labels (required for hosting demo)
1. Label AI nodes (8GB Pis):
   `docker node update --label-add role=ai pi-ai-1`
   `docker node update --label-add role=ai pi-ai-2`
2. Label general nodes (4GB Pis):
   `docker node update --label-add role=general pi-gen-1`
   `docker node update --label-add role=general pi-gen-2`
   `docker node update --label-add role=general pi-gen-3`
3. Verify labels:
   `docker node inspect pi-ai-1 --format '{{json .Spec.Labels}}'`
   `docker node inspect pi-gen-1 --format '{{json .Spec.Labels}}'`

Manager Pi Repo Setup
1. Clone repo:
   `cd ~ && git clone <your-repo-url> NasaHunch`
2. Install deployer/test dependencies (minimal):
   `cd ~/NasaHunch`
   `pip3 install --user pyyaml pytest`

Pre-Pull Public Images (recommended on all Pis to avoid delays)
Run on all Pis:
1. `docker pull prom/node-exporter:latest`
2. `docker pull prom/prometheus:latest`
3. `docker pull prom/pushgateway:latest`
4. `docker pull grafana/grafana-enterprise:11.1.1`
5. `docker pull nginx:stable-alpine`
6. `docker pull ollama/ollama:latest`

Build Custom Monitoring Stack Images (manager Pi)
Run from `~/NasaHunch/Cluster`:
1. `docker build -t cluster_sensor_exporter ./simulated_nodes`
2. `docker build -t cluster_backup_service ./backup_service`
3. `docker build -t cluster_disk_health ./disk_health`
4. `docker build -t cluster_manager ./cluster_manager`
5. `docker build -t cluster_test_stress ./test_stress`
6. `docker build -t cluster_log_aggregator ./log_aggregator`
7. `docker build -t cluster_backend ./backend`

Important note for custom images
1. If Swarm schedules a custom `cluster_*` image to a worker, that worker must be able to pull or already have that image.
2. For the presentation, safest options are:
   `A.` Keep services on manager if possible
   `B.` Use a local registry
   `C.` Build/load custom images on workers ahead of time
3. The hosting demo examples `test-web` and `test-ai-ollama` use public images and are safer.

Deploy Monitoring Stack
1. From `~/NasaHunch/Cluster`:
   `docker stack deploy -c docker-stack.yml cluster`
2. Verify:
   `docker stack services cluster`
   `docker service ls`

Quick Smoke Checks (do before audience arrives)
Run on manager Pi:
1. `docker node ls`
2. `docker stack services cluster`
3. `docker service ps cluster_node_exporter`
4. `docker service ps cluster_sensor_exporter`
5. `docker service ps cluster_prometheus`
6. `docker service logs cluster_sensor_exporter --tail 20`
7. `curl http://localhost:9200/metrics | head`
8. `curl http://localhost:9090/-/healthy`
9. Open `http://<manager-ip>:9090/targets` and confirm multiple `node_exporter` targets are up

Presentation Day "Quick Start" (copy/paste sequence)
1. `cd ~/NasaHunch/Cluster`
2. `docker stack deploy -c docker-stack.yml cluster`
3. `docker stack services cluster`
4. `docker service ps cluster_node_exporter`
5. `docker service ps cluster_prometheus`
6. `docker service ps cluster_sensor_exporter`
7. `cd ~/NasaHunch`
8. `python3 deployer/deployer.py --queue-dir queue --dry-run`

Presentation Runbook (fast flow)
1. Show cluster + node roles:
   `docker node ls`
   `docker node inspect pi-ai-1 --format '{{json .Spec.Labels}}'`
   `docker node inspect pi-gen-1 --format '{{json .Spec.Labels}}'`
2. Show monitoring stack:
   `cd ~/NasaHunch/Cluster`
   `docker stack services cluster`
   `docker service ps cluster_node_exporter`
   `docker service logs cluster_sensor_exporter --tail 20`
   `docker service logs cluster_prometheus --tail 20`
3. Show live metrics in browser (replace `<manager-ip>`):
   `http://<manager-ip>:9200/metrics`
   `http://<manager-ip>:9090/targets`
   `http://<manager-ip>:9090/graph` (query `sensor_temperature_c`, `sensor_humidity_pct`)
   `http://<manager-ip>:8000/register` (API route exists, expects POST)
   `http://<manager-ip>:8000/report_metrics` (API route exists, expects POST)
   `http://<manager-ip>:3000` (Grafana)
4. Show hosting functionality (safe first):
   `cd ~/NasaHunch`
   `python3 deployer/deployer.py --queue-dir queue --dry-run`
5. Show real hosting deployment:
   `python3 deployer/deployer.py --queue-dir queue`
6. Verify hosted services + scheduling:
   `docker service ls`
   `docker service ps test-web`
   `docker service ps test-ai-ollama`
   `docker service ps test-git-app`

What to point out during hosting demo
1. `sample_web.yaml` schedules to `role=general`.
2. `sample_ai.yaml` schedules to `role=ai`.
3. `sample_git_app.yaml` can build from a Git repo (dry-run is safest live demo for this one).
4. Check `NODE` column in `docker service ps` to prove placement.
5. If `test-git-app` fails live, continue with `test-web` and `test-ai-ollama` and show `sample_git_app` in dry-run.

Useful Logs / Debug Commands During Presentation
1. `docker stack services cluster`
2. `docker service ls`
3. `docker service ps cluster_node_exporter`
3. `docker service ps cluster_sensor_exporter`
4. `docker service ps cluster_prometheus`
5. `docker service logs cluster_sensor_exporter --tail 50`
6. `docker service logs cluster_prometheus --tail 50`
7. `docker service logs cluster_backend --tail 50`
8. `docker service logs test-web --tail 50`
9. `docker service logs test-ai-ollama --tail 50`
10. `docker service logs cluster_node_exporter --tail 20`

Prometheus Queries (real per-Pi metrics)
1. CPU usage by Pi:
   `100 - (avg by (instance) (rate(node_cpu_seconds_total{mode="idle"}[1m])) * 100)`
2. Memory usage percent by Pi:
   `(1 - (node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes / node_memory_MemTotal_bytes)) * 100`
3. Disk usage percent by Pi:
   `100 * (1 - (node_filesystem_avail_bytes{fstype!~"tmpfs|overlay"} / node_filesystem_size_bytes{fstype!~"tmpfs|overlay"}))`

Queue examples
1. `queue/sample_web.yaml` (web app image)
2. `queue/sample_ai.yaml` (AI app image, `role=ai` placement)
3. `queue/sample_git_app.yaml` (build image from Git repo)

Run unit tests (manager Pi, optional)
1. `pytest -v deployer/test_deployer.py`

Known Demo Caveats / Fallbacks
1. `sample_git_app.yaml` real deployment may fail on worker nodes if the built image exists only on the manager and no registry is used.
2. Safer live demo: real deploy `sample_web` + `sample_ai`, use `sample_git_app` in `--dry-run`.
3. `ollama/ollama:latest` can take time to pull on Pi; pre-pull it on both 8GB Pis if possible.
4. First-time image pulls/builds can be slow. Do them before the audience arrives.

If a service fails live (fast recovery)
1. Check task placement and error:
   `docker service ps <service-name>`
2. Check logs:
   `docker service logs <service-name> --tail 50`
3. Remove only the failing hosted service and continue:
   `docker service rm <service-name>`
4. Continue demo with monitoring + successful hosted services.

Cleanup After Demo
1. `docker stack rm cluster`
2. `docker service rm test-web test-ai-ollama test-git-app`
